#
# Scenario (0): negative control - both priority options are
# omitted, so no "redo log thread priority:" line is
# printed at all, and all six redo log threads keep whatever
# nice value they inherited from the process (compared against
# the observed nice of the main thread, not a hardcoded 0 - MTR
# itself may not start mysqld at nice 0).
#
# restart:
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (0), step 0: area after this restart contains a completed start]
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (0): no redo log thread priority line while both options are omitted]
#
# Scenario (1): both sysvars set, no correction needed - exactly
# 4 threads (writer, flusher, write_notifier, flush_notifier) get
# the writers' nice, exactly 2 (checkpointer, files_governor) get
# the checkpoint group's. Both values are above the nice observed
# in scenario (0): raising a nice needs no privilege, lowering one
# does, so a test that only raises runs as any user.
#
# restart
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (1): SYSTEM line reports the applied priorities]
#
# Scenario (2): checkpoint priority not strictly above writers' -
# kept as configured, with a WARNING line, plus the
# usual SYSTEM line (two "redo log thread priority:" lines total).
#
call mtr.add_suppression("redo log thread priority");
# restart
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (2): WARNING line reports the priority ordering]
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (2): exactly one WARNING and one SYSTEM line, no more]
#
# Scenario (3): final restart back to default options - required
# for the mutation gate, which needs the next test in the
# queue to be able to reuse this server: while $restart_opts is
# left set (a non-default my.cnf line still applied), MTR always
# restarts the next queued test, and check_testcase() also does
# not run for a server with $restart_opts set. The bare "restart:"
# form (colon, nothing after) clears $restart_opts entirely - see
# mysql-test-run.pl's parsing of the .expect file. Nothing is
# asserted here on purpose.
#
# restart:
